AppDR citations and design references
Last updated: 2026-05-15

Important scope note
- AppDR is a thesis screening-support prototype. It is not an FDA-cleared or clinically validated diagnostic device.
- Commercial DR systems such as IDx-DR and EyeArt use validated clinical workflows, high-quality fundus cameras, and trained machine/deep-learning systems. Their proprietary algorithms were not copied.
- The current AppDR backend uses classical image processing plus an optional trainable feature-based classifier.

FDA-cleared DR detection systems
1. U.S. Food and Drug Administration. Device Classification Under Section 513(f)(2)(De Novo): IDx-DR, DEN180001. Decision date: 2018-04-11.
   URL: https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/cdrh/cfdocs/cfpmn/denovo.cfm?id=DEN180001
   Notes for thesis: IDx-DR is listed as a diabetic retinopathy detection device. The FDA decision summary reports key performance results including sensitivity 87%, specificity 90%, imageability 96%, PPV 73%, and NPV 96%.

2. U.S. Food and Drug Administration. 510(k) Premarket Notification: EyeArt, K200667. Decision date: 2020-08-03.
   URL: https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/cdrh/cfdocs/cfpmn/pmn.cfm?ID=K200667
   Summary PDF: https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/cdrh_docs/pdf20/K200667.pdf
   Notes for thesis: EyeArt is indicated to detect more-than-mild DR and vision-threatening DR from color fundus images captured by specified fundus cameras. The FDA summary describes use of an ensemble of clinically aligned machine-learning/deep-learning algorithms, image quality handling, and clinical validation against reading-center grading.

Classical image-processing concepts used by AppDR
3. Niemeijer, M., van Ginneken, B., Staal, J., Suttorp-Schulten, M. S. A., & Abramoff, M. D. Automatic detection of microaneurysms in color fundus images. IEEE Transactions on Medical Imaging, 2005/2007 indexed record.
   URL: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17950655/
   Notes for thesis: Supports the use of green-channel preprocessing, shade correction, normalization, and candidate filtering for microaneurysm detection.

4. Semi-automated quantification of hard exudates in colour fundus photographs diagnosed with diabetic retinopathy.
   URL: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5607622/
   Notes for thesis: Supports exudate-oriented image-processing steps such as top-hat filtering, statistical filtering, and thresholding.

5. Decencière, E., Zhang, X., Cazuguel, G., et al. Feedback on a publicly distributed image database: the Messidor database. Image Analysis & Stereology, 33(3), 231, 2014. DOI: 10.5566/ias.1155.
   URL: https://www.researchgate.net/publication/272989606_FEEDBACK_ON_A_PUBLICLY_DISTRIBUTED_IMAGE_DATABASE_THE_MESSIDOR_DATABASE
   Notes for thesis: Messidor is a commonly used public fundus database for evaluating automatic lesion segmentation and DR grading methods.

Public datasets for training and validation
6. APTOS 2019 Blindness Detection. Kaggle competition/dataset, 2019.
   URL: https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/aptos2019-blindness-detection
   Notes for thesis: Contains retinal fundus images labeled from 0 to 4 according to DR severity. Useful for training and testing, subject to Kaggle terms.

7. EyePACS / Kaggle Diabetic Retinopathy Detection Challenge, 2015.
   URL: https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/diabetic-retinopathy-detection
   Notes for thesis: Large fundus-image dataset used in DR detection research. Useful for deep-learning or feature-model training if access is available.

8. Indian Diabetic Retinopathy Image Dataset (IDRiD).
   URL: https://ieee-dataport.org/open-access/indian-diabetic-retinopathy-image-dataset-idrid
   Notes for thesis: Includes fundus images and DR-related lesion annotations, useful for validating lesion localization algorithms.

How these references influenced AppDR
- Input quality gate: follows the validated-system idea that images can be ungradable, but avoids rejecting full-frame fundus crops only because the retinal circular border is not visible.
- Preprocessing: green-channel enhancement, illumination correction, and CLAHE-style local contrast enhancement.
- Vessel handling: vessel-like structures are segmented and excluded from red-lesion candidate masks to reduce false microaneurysm and hemorrhage detections.
- Bright lesion handling: top-hat style bright candidate detection is restricted by color and optic-disc exclusion to reduce false exudate detections.
- Scoring: current probability is a screening score from extracted features. It is not a clinical probability unless trained and validated on labeled images.
